<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_elec-wire" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Application: The Charged Wire</title>

	<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section were designed to be implemented in a 50-minute interactive classroom, after students have completed the rest of the chapter, especially:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_integration"/>
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_semicircular-wire"/>
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_calculating-e"/>
			</p>
		</li>
	</ul>
  </assemblage>

  <subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>
	
  </subsubsection>

	<activity xml:id="class_charged-wire">
		<title>The Charged Wire</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			A total charge <m>+q</m> is spread uniformly over a wire of length <m>L</m>.  You want to determine the electric field at the indicated point.
		</p>
		<figure xml:id="fig_elec-wire">
		<title>Wire Figure</title>
    	<caption>A charged wire.</caption>
    		<image source="elec-wire.png" width = "80%" >
          <description>A horizontal wire of length L.  A point is labeled a distance y above the center.</description>
        </image>
    </figure>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<alert>Predict:</alert> What direction do you expect the electric field to point?  Do you expect its magnitude to be bigger, smaller or equal to <m>\frac{kq}{y^2}</m> ?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<alert>Chop</alert> the object up into pieces.  Label at least one <m>dq</m> and identify relevant quantities.  What is <m>dq</m> in terms of known quantities?  What is <m>r</m>?  What is <m>\hat{r}</m>?

				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<alert>Multiply</alert> the relevant quantities together to find <m>\vec{dE}</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<alert>Add</alert> the <m>\vec{dE}</m> over the entire wire!
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<alert>Make sense</alert> of your answer.  Do the units check out?  What happens as <m>L \rightarrow 0</m>?  As <m>y \rightarrow \inf</m>?  As <m>y \rightarrow 0</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<hint>
				<p>
					Remember to state: (a) Your expectation of what will happen, (b) a physical description why that should happen, and (c) a verification that your expression matches your expectation.

				</p>
			</hint>
		</task>
	</activity>

